{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) to justify preferring better-corroborated theories without induction. However, Popper's own formal attempt to define verisimilitude failed because it logically requires comparing the truth-content and falsity-content of theories, which necessitates inductive assumptions about the structure of truth and the likelihood that a theory surviving severe tests is closer to the truth than a falsified one. By invoking verisimilitude as the grounding mechanism, the target smuggles in the very inductive logic Popper sought to reject, failing to provide a strictly non-inductive justification.","premise":"Popper's concept of verisimilitude cannot be formally defined or operationalized without relying on inductive assumptions about the relationship between surviving tests and proximity to truth.","premises_essential":["d1d5f9b1501e0b319e85528a2c8cc757981e07539e060001611fa53fe991da36"],"successor_question":"If verisimilitude is rejected as an inductive concept, what specific non-inductive principle allows a Popperian to prefer one unfalsified theory over another when both have survived the same tests?","target":"d1d5f9b1501e0b319e85528a2c8cc757981e07539e060001611fa53fe991da36"}]}